WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.570 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I've talked to Trump maybe
more recently than ever.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.539 align:start size:84% position:16%
Same guy, but I
don't agree with him.

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:09.309
I don't agree with the pulling
out of our troops outta Syria

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:10.343
and abandoning the Kurds.

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:12.379
I don't agree with the down way

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:14.381 align:start size:88% position:13%
we're pulling out of
Afghanistan at the time,

00:00:14.381 --> 00:00:17.684
I didn't want 5,000 troops,
but I thought 1200 was fine.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
I disagree with
them on a few things

00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:23.123 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he didn't like that,
but I think he respected it.

00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:25.625
But the audience goes crazy,

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.160
especially early
when you disagree

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.262 align:start size:94% position:6%
with what the president's
doing that you know,

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:30.964
General Mattis is
not a bad General.

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:32.198
It's not a good thing to say

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.067
I like people that
didn't get captured.

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:35.468
Doesn't mean I hate Trump,

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:37.837
but I am not going
to pretend to saying

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:39.806
that about John
McCain was right.

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:48.982 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm Dave Rubin
and joining me today

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:51.284
is the co-anchor
of Fox &amp; Friends,

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:53.920
the host of the Brian
Kilmeade radio show,

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.922 align:start size:81% position:19%
the host of One
Nation on Fox News

00:00:55.922 --> 00:01:00.326 align:start position:0%
and a New York Times bestselling
author, Brian Kilmeade.

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:03.096
Brian, finally, welcome
to the Ruben Report.

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:04.564
- Yeah, nice to see you, Dave.

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:05.932
- That is quite a resume.

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:07.734
You know, sometimes
we bring on guests,

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:10.203 align:start size:84% position:16%
it could be a kind of
thin resume. I gotta
get to it easily,

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:12.038
but you do a lot of stuff.

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:15.075 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think that's point
being driven home by the fact

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.476
that I think you're
standing right now,

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:18.912
like you gotta go in a second.

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:20.814
- Right. No, I prefer
the standing desk.

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:22.816 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I know you talk about
these guys with thin resume's,

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:25.051 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's because you're talking
about Joe Rogan, right?

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:28.221
That guy doesn't really do much
and you have trouble finding

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.789
a reason to interview him.

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:32.559 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yes, he's not
doing much out there,

00:01:32.559 --> 00:01:36.329
but you have like 87 shows.
People see you all the time.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:38.264
You also, you're on
Gutfeld all the time.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:40.200
You're guest hosting
on every other show.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.734
- With you.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:44.471
- Yeah, we're doing the show
together, all that good stuff.

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:45.705
But I'm curious,

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:47.040 align:start size:84% position:16%
I actually don't
know a ton about you

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.675
beyond your Fox life.

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:51.077 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was curious just a little
bit about like history,

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:53.480
childhood growing up,
what got you into this,

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.282
this crazy media situation?

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:57.951
- So Dave, it's really
none of your business,

00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:00.386
so I just wish you'd back off

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:01.554
and stop the personal questions.

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:02.388
- Sorry man,

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:03.323
This is Barbara Walters?

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:04.824
I mean, what's going on here?

00:02:04.824 --> 00:02:06.860
- We wanna make you cry by the
end of this thing, you know?

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:07.694
- Yes, I am.

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:10.497
I am crying inside.

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:15.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
Basically, my big
thing was growing up,

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:17.303
I was a big time soccer player.

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:18.505
Wanted to be great, wasn't.

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:22.008
My whole family, my whole town.

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:25.044
Went to college, played soccer
at Post, but the whole time,

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:27.147 align:start size:97% position:3%
by the time I got to
college, it was pretty clear.

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:29.349 align:start size:84% position:16%
Not only wasn't
there a pro league,

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:31.618
but I wasn't nearly
in the conversation

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:33.853 align:start size:97% position:3%
with anything like that,
although all my friends were,

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:35.989
which is certainly humbling.

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:38.224 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then I always
wanted to do radio, TV,

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:39.792
really since ninth grade.

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.461
And that's what I was
always pushing for,

00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:43.229
whether it was at the
campus radio station

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:44.731
or interning at NBC.

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:47.400
Doing the history of
late night television

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.302
as my senior project.

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:50.603 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've got an interview
with Letterman

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:52.539
when I was still in college.

00:02:52.539 --> 00:02:54.874 align:start size:97% position:3%
Not for a job, but actually
for the air, for radio.

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:56.409
- Oh wow.

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:57.677 align:start size:94% position:6%
- And that's what I wanted
to do and I went right,

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
took all that momentum
and all that drive

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:02.081 align:start size:97% position:3%
and went right to Bennigan's
where I kept waitering

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:05.018 align:start size:94% position:6%
right after college trying
to get that first job.

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:08.154 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I started my own local
show, got a bunch of sponsors,

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:09.789 align:start size:84% position:16%
got a limo deal,
got a clothing deal.

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:11.291
- Wow.

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:14.494 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Got a catering deal, got
a little sponsor money,

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:18.631
built a set and realized,
because in our business

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:20.833
it was pre podcast days,
if you don't have a tape

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:22.302
you're not gonna get a job.

00:03:22.302 --> 00:03:24.237 align:start size:94% position:6%
They don't care about
your school, your grades.

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:27.707 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I was able to get a tape
then work on my accent,

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.409 align:start size:84% position:16%
started doing
standup along the way

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:30.610
because that'll
be an opportunity

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.278
that no one could deny you.

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:33.980
There's always a
stage to go up on,

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.281
even if it's a terrible one

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:37.417
in the middle of a
diner at 5:00 PM.

00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:38.618
- Yep.

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:40.253
So it'd be a great
way to get composure,

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:42.255
be able to react to an audience.

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:46.192
And then went out to LA and
got a job at a TV station

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:49.662
doing all sports radio on the
weekends and doing standup.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:54.367 align:start size:84% position:16%
Probably making
$27,000 all in all.

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:55.635
You know, I remember
I was getting paid

00:03:55.635 --> 00:03:59.906 align:start size:84% position:16%
596 every two
weeks to work in TV

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:02.976 align:start size:84% position:16%
at which was these,
what happens is these,

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:05.311
the home shopping network
bought a bunch of stations

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:07.780
so they can get on cable
and to have just get on,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.749 align:start size:84% position:16%
they had to form their
own news service.

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:13.253
So I'd go in there with one
news director and two people

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:15.688
and we'd do all the copy,
do all the interviews,

00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:19.259
do all the booking. So it was
a great way to get experience.

00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:24.264
And then got a job back here
in New York for $35,000 a year

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:29.302
doing sports and I was doing
that and was great to be home.

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:33.072
The only thing bad about it,
after three months I got fired.

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:36.142
The new guy they hired
was actually the anchor

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:37.510 align:start size:81% position:19%
masquerading as
the news director.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.679 align:start size:84% position:16%
The news director
became the sports guy.

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:42.415
And then I'm out of a
job and then was able

00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:44.884 align:start size:81% position:19%
to claw my way
back into Newsport

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:48.988
get an interview with Fox
in 96, started filling in.

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:50.923
And I've been able to hold
onto the job ever since.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:54.127 align:start size:84% position:16%
I got extremely
lucky after 12 years.

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:55.228
- Yeah, that's what
they always say.

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:56.496
It's an overnight success.

00:04:56.496 --> 00:04:58.364
But where the hell were
you for those first?

00:04:58.364 --> 00:04:59.299
- Yeah.

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:00.967
- You know, 12, 15, 20 years.

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:02.935
Do you miss a little
bit of that kind of,

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:04.404 align:start size:84% position:16%
I didn't realize
that you had started

00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:05.905 align:start size:84% position:16%
a bunch of things
yourself and all that

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:07.674 align:start size:81% position:19%
and as you said
before podcasting.

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:09.175 align:start size:84% position:16%
Do you miss a little
of that grind?

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:10.910
Because you're in a pretty
smooth system right now.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:12.945
Whenever I do anything with Fox

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:14.447
and I saw you at
the Patriot Awards,

00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:17.984
it's like Fox just does
a nice job of programming

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:18.785
and it's good and it works.

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:19.619
- Yeah.

00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:20.853
- And it's, yeah.

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:22.355
Do you miss any of
that? The messiness?

00:05:22.355 --> 00:05:23.589 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well a couple of things that
I was able to do, you know,

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:25.391
like the scramble where
you're out on your own

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:26.859
like you were doing,

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:29.362 align:start position:0%
when you go and do speaking
events and you get people there.

00:05:29.362 --> 00:05:30.897
I have a little of
that with the books.

00:05:30.897 --> 00:05:32.265
So when the books come out,

00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:35.435 align:start position:0%
it's up to us to chart
something that Fox is gonna okay

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:37.403
that allows us to go do
personal appearances.

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:40.807 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you try to get out
there to different theaters

00:05:40.807 --> 00:05:44.711 align:start size:97% position:3%
or different events, even
breweries and you try to get

00:05:44.711 --> 00:05:47.113
500 people there to
do a book signing.

00:05:47.113 --> 00:05:49.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or you go to a theater
and you try to get

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:52.518 align:start size:81% position:19%
between 500 and
1200 people there.

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:53.953
And I'll talk about
all my books at once.

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:57.557 align:start position:0%
So that's a little of the
grind and the entrepreneurship.

00:05:57.557 --> 00:06:00.326
But within the one thing in
Fox, I think you picked up,

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:02.662 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, Fox &amp;
Friends is a machine.

00:06:02.662 --> 00:06:03.863
But within those interviews,

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:05.565
if I have Dave Rubin one-on one,

00:06:05.565 --> 00:06:08.301
I do what I want and I
talk to the producers.

00:06:08.301 --> 00:06:11.204 align:start size:94% position:6%
If I have, you know,
Trey Gowdy has a book out

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:13.940
or Mike Pompeo has a book out,

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:15.174
you know, confer
with the producers.

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:16.509
But pretty much you
do what you want.

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:17.810
But that's a machine.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.779
Like Fox &amp; Friends is
a logo for a reason.

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:22.582
It's gonna be successful
with or without the talent,

00:06:22.582 --> 00:06:23.750
no matter who it is,

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.451 align:start size:84% position:16%
they'll get the
right people in there

00:06:25.451 --> 00:06:27.453 align:start size:88% position:13%
because they know
exactly what they want.

00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:30.289
But the radio show I feel as
though within that three hours

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:31.457
I could do what I want.

00:06:32.859 --> 00:06:34.360 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, there's people that
we can't book 'cause it's

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.629
you know, I'm not gonna see if
Anderson Cooper's available,

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:38.398
you know, the rivals.

00:06:38.398 --> 00:06:39.565
But for the most part
you do what you want.

00:06:39.565 --> 00:06:41.467
And then on the weekend show,

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:44.904 align:start position:0%
I really feel like the Saturday
show at 8:00 Eastern is,

00:06:44.904 --> 00:06:46.906
it's kind of my thing
with great producers

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:48.474
and everyone collaborates.

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:51.477
So within this great machine,

00:06:51.477 --> 00:06:52.979
the New York Yankees,

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:54.547 align:start size:94% position:6%
before anyone knew they
were the New York Yankees,

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:56.048
you can be yourself.

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:57.583 align:start size:84% position:16%
So that's the
grind a little bit.

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:59.852
And I just feel so
relieved every day.

00:06:59.852 --> 00:07:02.121
So Dave, we meet so
many talented people

00:07:02.121 --> 00:07:03.556
that don't have great jobs,

00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:05.057
even though they're great.

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:07.126
So a lot of it is opportunity.

00:07:07.126 --> 00:07:09.328 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the only thing I'm
gonna give myself credit for

00:07:09.328 --> 00:07:12.865 align:start position:0%
is knowing how great this
opportunity was and not thinking

00:07:12.865 --> 00:07:15.868 align:start size:84% position:16%
there was something
the grass was greener.

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:18.204
'Cause after those 12 years

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:20.106 align:start size:84% position:16%
and talking to people
while I'm here,

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:22.608
I know that grass
is not greener.

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:24.644 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, it's interesting
because, you know,

00:07:24.644 --> 00:07:27.346
I feel like I know you pretty
well a little bit off camera

00:07:27.346 --> 00:07:29.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
but mostly on camera
and through radio

00:07:29.048 --> 00:07:30.483
and all that kind of stuff.

00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:32.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we've been doing
this for 8 or 10 minutes,

00:07:32.819 --> 00:07:34.620 align:start size:88% position:13%
you haven't said
anything about politics.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.989 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I wonder do you consider
yourself really like

00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:40.126
a political animal or
more of a TV animal?

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.430 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I mean, if you ask
me, Brian format a show,

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.899 align:start size:97% position:3%
you don't have to, you
know, Fox just wants a show.

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:48.668
I love the politics of it.

00:07:48.668 --> 00:07:49.936
I was glad to leave sports.

00:07:49.936 --> 00:07:51.304
Like I like busy.

00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:53.139
You know, it's good
to like a team again.

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:55.808 align:start position:0%
When you do sports, I don't
know how the other people feel,

00:07:55.808 --> 00:07:57.510
but after a while it was a job.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:58.711 align:start size:88% position:13%
I wasn't really
enjoying going to games.

00:07:58.711 --> 00:08:00.146
- Yeah.

00:08:00.146 --> 00:08:01.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Hey, the Mets are
playing seven 30 tonight,

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:03.249
you can go cover the game.
Like, aren't you a Met fan?

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:05.351
I'm like, yeah, but you know,

00:08:05.351 --> 00:08:07.186
I don't really need to
go deal with the traffic.

00:08:07.186 --> 00:08:09.255
Go back and forth. It
took the fun out of it.

00:08:09.255 --> 00:08:11.224 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, but I love
doing the politics.

00:08:11.224 --> 00:08:14.327 align:start size:97% position:3%
I love it because it's a
lot like sports as you know.

00:08:14.327 --> 00:08:15.862
What's the strategy?

00:08:15.862 --> 00:08:17.430
What are you gonna
do to support it?

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:18.798
you know, so instead of fans,

00:08:18.798 --> 00:08:21.200 align:start size:84% position:16%
you got money to
support the candidate

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.037 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then you have the issues
and besides the issues,

00:08:25.037 --> 00:08:26.572
what are you gonna do
to get out the vote?

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:29.275
There's so many different
elements that I'm into.

00:08:29.275 --> 00:08:30.510
So you have these
great candidates

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:31.978
but couldn't get
their message out.

00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:34.680 align:start size:94% position:6%
Great candidates that
couldn't get any support.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then the
phenomenon of Trump,

00:08:36.382 --> 00:08:38.684
which no one's ever
gonna figure out.

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:39.919
Whether you love
him or hate him,

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:41.954
no one's ever seen
anything like this.

00:08:41.954 --> 00:08:43.556
And that to me,

00:08:43.556 --> 00:08:45.892
to be able to go talk to
Trump and get a chance

00:08:45.892 --> 00:08:48.294 align:start size:84% position:16%
to meet these
people and you know,

00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:51.631
